\f0\fs24 \cf0 Adding wocrldpop to Mexico City hospot project\
\
Using street boundaries and crime observations already prepped:\
	so we expect these files in data/street/processed folder:\
		segments.geoson (contains 877 street segments)\
		audit.json (identifies the study boundary and source dates)\
		events.csv (contains crime records)\
		neighbors.csv (ident. Connected streets)\
The use commands to reproduce pop addition using those saved inputs.\
In vs code: cd Mexico-crime\
Find pop data in World Pop Mexico catalog. In api documentation\
The selected dataset is WorldPop Global2 R2025A (annual pop grid estimates on a grid space approximately 100m apart)\
	The downloads are: Mexico catalog, API documetation, dataset citation\
Used 2 api endpoints:\
	GET https://api.worldpop.org/v3/countries/MEX/files?year=2023\
		that retrieves the year\'92s Mexico file catalog and source info\
	POST https://api.worldpop.org/v3/raster\
		that requests a pop raster for our geographic  area\
The request body follows this structure. The script calculates request_area from our street geometry \
	payload = \{\
		\'93geojson\'94: request_area,\
		\'93year\'94: year,\
		\'93resolution\'94: \'93100m\'94,\
		\'93layer\'94: \'93population\'94,\
	\}\
The api returns a GeoTIFF, a geographic image whose pixels contains pop values\
The install the raster-reading package\
Add rasterio to the street analysis reqs. It reads the downloaded pop maps.\
The existing packages handle tables, geometry, coordinate conversion, and model training.\
To install everything required by this part of the project:\
	street-analysis/.venv/bin/pip install -r street-analysis/requirements.txt\
Use the street analysis\'92s own python environment so activation is optional when using these full executable paths.\
Then run the population importer.\
Write population.py\
Run it with:\
	street-analysis/.venv/bin/python street-analysis/population.py\
		\'97start-year 2019 \\\
		\'97end-year 2026\
The script does these steps:\
	reads our street geometries\
	converts coordinates to EPSG:32614 where distances and areas can be calculated  in meters\
	draws a 200 m buffer around each street segment\
	requests pop rasters covering those buffers and the study boundary\
	calculates the estimated pop inside each buffer\
	saves annual pop features and source metadata\
It downloads 2018-2026. 2018 helps us calculate the change from 2018 to 2019\
The buffer construction is implemented with:\
buffers = [\
	line.buffer(200, quad_segs=16)\
	for line in lines\
]\
A buffer extends 200 m around the full street geometry\
When a pop cell partly overlaps a buffer, the script assigns the corresponding fraction of its pop\
	population_contribution = (\
		cell_population\
		* overlap_area\
		/ cell_area\
	)\
So if a cell contains an estimated 100 residents and half its area fall inside the buffer, it contributes 50 residents.\
So pop is distributed uniformly within that individual cell.\
The create the pop features.\
For every street and year, the importer creates these values:\
population_200m is estimated residents inside the street buffer\
population_density_200m is estimated residents divided by buffer area in km^2\
population_change_pct is percentage change from the previous year\
buffer_area_km2 is area used for the calculation\
The annual change is calculated with:\
	change_pct = (\
		current_population / previous_population - 1\
	) * 100\
The resulting table has 7,016 rows which is 877 streets * by 8 years.\
The importer saves:\
data/street/raw/worldpop/\
    Downloaded GeoTIFFs and request metadata\
\
data/street/processed/population_features.csv\
    Annual population features for each street\
\
data/street/processed/population.json\
    Source information, checksums, settings, and study totals\
Rerunning the importer reuses the cached files. It checks their hashes and verifies the dataset release, year, units, and grid resolutions.\
The join pop to the monthly crime observations.\
Update train.py to join the pop table using street ID and forecast-origin year.\
	the relevant operation:\
	panel.assign(\
		population_year=panel.month.dt.year\
	).merge(\
		population,\
		on=[\'93segment_id\'94, \'93population_year\'94],\
		how=\'93left\'94,\
		validate=\'93many_to_one\'94,\
	)\
So for example, a street\'92s March 2024 observation receives that street\'92s 2024 pop estimate. The annual estimate repeats across the months of that year.\
The crime info still changes months, pop supplies annual context\
The retrain and compare the models.\
Run: street-analysis/.venv/bin/python street-analysis/train.py --population\
The \'97population at the end adds three comparisons:\
	random forest with neighbors and KDE\
	random forest with neighbors, KDE, pop\
	XGBoost with nieghbors, KDE, pop\
The additional random forest + KDE model is a matching control for its pop variant\
The models use the same 3 chronological test periods as before:\
1 - March to August 2025\
2 - Sept 2025 to Feb 2026\
3 - March to Aug 2026\
Each experiment trains on earlier observations and evals later observations.\
Together the tests contain 15,786 street-month rows and 484 matched crime records\
Also add eval by pop-density group. To see performance across areas with different densities.\
The results of this were published to postgreSQL and the projects db connection configured:\
	run: street-analysis/.venv/bin/python street-analysis/publish.py\
The pop data goes into a new street_population table. The script also publishes updated model results.\
These changes run within a db transaction.\
Then, build and run the dashboard.\
Add pop endpoint to street_api.py and update StreetApp.jsx\
New endpoint:\
	GET /api/street/population?year=2020\
The dash uses it to display pop density around streets with a year selector and details for the selected street.\
The page also shows annual pop estimates and evaluation results by density groups.\
Build frontend:\
	cd frontend\
	nom run build\
Start app:\
	cd mexico-crime\
	sh dashboard/start.sh\
To verify:\
Run an automated test:\
	cd mexico-crime\
	PYTHONPATH=street-analysis \\ \
	street_analysis/.venv/bin/python -m unites discover \\\
		-s street-analysis/tests\
These include checks that partial-cell calculations conserve a pop counts and that mission pop data triggers an error.\
While dash in running, in another terminal run:\
	cd mexico-crime\
	dashboard/.venv/bin/python street-analysis/check_api.py\
That will check pop coverage, the added predictions, density-group counts, and existing dash funcs.\
